    "q-3922": {
      "dom_id": "step-q3922",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}\\sqrt{x-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-4}.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =\\sqrt{4-4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253135/assets/4c434365084b3123c313d5de1ddd0692e1658b6fcc6b7c03869cb2cebf94253e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we notice that for the left-hand limit, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\n$$\n\nFor the right-hand limit, we have:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=0\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are not equal, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
