      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{-(x+7)}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{-(x+7)}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253135/assets/fcd2e8fce7bacf19fc5dd4f65dbee568293ed7ee54f7c4542fdb65d85aaba7d8.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
